WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.327
Most examples in this module depend on the same relationship.

00:00:03.327 --> 00:00:07.797
A patient can have many admissions, many lab results, and many prescriptions.

00:00:07.797 --> 00:00:13.127
When you see a correlated condition such as a dot patient ID equals p dot patient ID,

00:00:13.127 --> 00:00:16.192
it is connecting a child row to the current patient row.

00:00:16.192 --> 00:00:22.592
Keeping the relationship map simple lets us focus on the subquery behavior rather than on a complicated

00:00:22.592 --> 00:00:27.597
schema. Connect this idea to a compliance workflow checking whether related evidence exists.

00:00:27.597 --> 00:00:32.502
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:32.502 --> 00:00:39.302
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:39.302 --> 00:00:44.432
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:44.432 --> 00:00:49.440
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
